CHAPTER 13
The Erie Problem
We now turn to a topic anticipated by our cases on venue, transfer, and forum non conveniens: the question of which law a court should use to decide the case before it. This is known as a choice-of-law question.

A federal court in a diversity action faces a particular kind of choice of law question. In a diversity action, the plaintiff’s complaint does seek relief under the U.S. Constitution, statutes, or treaties. (If it did, federal question would be a basis for subject matter jurisdiction.) For example, if the plaintiff alleges a common law tort or breach of contract, which law should the federal judge turn to when deciding that claim? Historically, federal courts have given two different answers.

First, the judge could select whatever she thought was the best formulation of the common law of tort or contract. The judge might find guidance in other federal court decisions or in the decisions of one or more state courts. (This view of the common law treats it as a single body of law common to all courts.) This approach was the approach taken by federal courts for the first century and a half of their existence and is exemplified by the first case in this chapter, Swift v. Tyson.

Second, for purposes of this question, the judge could treat her court as if it were a court of a specific state and follow whatever case law the judges of that state would follow. (This view of the common law treats each state’s judge-made law as a distinct set of legal commands, specific to that state.) This approach is what federal courts do today. Erie Railroad v. Tompkins, our second case, is the seminal case on this question. Erie was decided in 1938, the same year that the Federal Rules came into effect. And, like the Federal Rules, Erie marked a decisive break with the past.

