As we will see, Erie resolved some of the ambiguities and complications of the Swift rule but raised far more questions than it answered. These questions, with which federal courts have wrestled for nearly a century, are known as the Erie problem. The Erie problem is a problem of vertical choice of law. By “vertical choice of law,” I mean the choice between using federal law or state law to resolve an issue before the court. (This is in contrast to questions of horizontal choice of law, which refer to the choice among the laws of different states to govern an issue before the court. Horizontal choice of law is beyond the scope of this book. It is the primary subject of courses and books, which are often titled conflict of laws.) Vertical choice-of-law questions extend far beyond the specific example this chapter began with. For example, to say that a federal judge should act as if she were a state judge when defining the elements of a tort does not answer the question of which state’s judge the federal judge should pretend to be. And the Erie problem raises the question not merely of whether a federal court should follow state tort law or contract law. What about state rules of procedure? Does Erie apply to procedure?

Indeed, the Erie problem poses a question with which this book began: What is procedure? As we shall study in detail, Erie requires federal courts to use state law as “rules of decision” in diversity cases. But, as we have seen throughout this book, federal courts follow federal procedural rules in all cases, including diversity cases. Thus, the Erie problem requires judges to distinguish between substantive law—the “rules of decision” that define the parties’ claims and defenses and determine who prevails in the case—and procedural law—the rules for how courts go about determining and enforcing the rights and duties created by substantive law. In this chapter, we will encounter legal issues that don’t fit neatly in only the substantive or only the procedural category.

For example, consider a state statute of limitations for tort claims. It says that, in order to recover on a tort claim, a person must file suit within two years of discovering their injury. Is this procedure, because it defines rules for the filing of civil actions? Or is it substantive law, because it determines whether or not a plaintiff can obtain damages for an injury? (We will revisit this question when we read Guaranty Trust Co. of New York v. York.) In this way, the Erie problem forces courts to address the question: “What is procedure?”

But first, we begin with the pathmarking cases that pose the fundamental questions this chapter will explore. Swift v. Tyson and its antithesis, Erie Railroad v. Tompkins, set the stage for all that follows. To Justice Joseph Story in
