A Brooding Omnipresence
To understand the Erie problem, we begin with the rule that Erie replaced. Given that the Constitution created federal courts separate from state courts, and federal law separate from state courts, American courts have had to confront choice-of-law questions since the dawn of the Republic.

Let’s explore the choice-of-law issues faced by federal courts in the nineteenth century and early twentieth century. Federal courts hearing cases alleging claims under federal statutes, treaties, or the U.S. Constitution have a straightforward choice-of-law problem: they will decide the case under that federal law. Federal courts hearing diversity cases face a potentially more difficult choice of law question. By definition, a diversity case does not arise under federal law. Thus, the question becomes under what law will this case be decided? If the complaint alleges violations of a state constitution, statute, or regulation, then the choice-of-law question is again easy: decide the case under the state law cited by the complaint.

But what if the claim is not grounded in any constitution, statute, or regulation? What if there is no codified law upon which the claim is grounded? In other words, what if the complaint alleges a right to relief based on the common law?

A traditional view of the common law was that (as the name suggests) it was “common”—shared by all courts. In this way, the general common law was a shared project to which all courts contributed, with each case offering another set of facts and statements of law to be compared with all other precedents by future judges.* From this point of view, if a federal court complaint invoked the common law, there was no choice-of-law conundrum, since there was only one common law to choose from.

But things are never quite that simple! The common law is not codified, and a judge’s understanding of what the common law requires can evolve over time as case law develops. And nothing requires two judges to read the same precedents the same way, or even to cite the same precedents when deciding a case. The nature of stare decisis, of legal research, and of judicial collegiality is such that the judges of a state are much more likely to follow precedents from
1209
their own state than from other states. What that means is that judges in New York may describe common law rules in different terms than judges in Pennsylvania. Even if the common law was, in some sense, common to all places, the reality was also that the case law expounding the common law in New York courts did not say the same thing as the case law of the Pennsylvania courts.

If you are federal judge in New York deciding a diversity case alleging common law causes of action, what do you do? Perhaps the thing to do is to decide the case under the general common law—in other words, without reference to the differences between the case laws of different states. The federal court would exercise its independent judgment about what the “right” answer was under the general common law.

But perhaps the federal district court sitting in New York, recognizing that different courts reach different results when applying the common law, would just pick one state’s case law and follow that. (Presumably, the judge would pick New York law.) And these two options aren’t incompatible. A federal judge can embrace the idea of a general common law, but as a practical matter just look to the local state courts for guidance on the “true” meaning of the general common law. And that is, as a general matter, what the federal courts did in the years before 1938.

But what if the federal judge thought that the local state courts had gotten the common law wrong? Could the federal court ignore the state courts of the state in which it sits and make an independent judgment? This was the question raised by Swift v. Tyson.

